WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.640
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.480
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.280
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.040
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.200
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.000
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.800
Today we look
at the safety

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.800
of fourth generation
sweeteners

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.777
such as the rare
sugar allulose.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.960
First there was sugar and
high fructose corn syrup,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.720
the original industrial sweeteners.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.240
They were cheap, but had
lots of empty calories,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.480
and contributed to diseases

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.040
such as obesity, type 2 diabetes,
cavities, and metabolic syndrome.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.320
So then came the second-generation
sweeteners, the artificial sweeteners,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.000
the colored pastel packets:
Nutrasweet, Splenda,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.640
Sweet &amp; Low.
Practically zero calories,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.120
but adverse effects have been
described, cautions have been raised.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.720
For example, artificial sweeteners were
found to induce glucose intolerance

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.920
by altering the gut microbiome,
thereby producing

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.760
the counterintuitive effect of
inducing metabolic derangements.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.720
They also taste kind of funny.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.960
Enter the third-generation
Sweeteners, sugar alcohols,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.680
such as sorbitol, xylitol, and
erythritol, low-calorie sweeteners,

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.800
however, their laxative effects
can create difficulties,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.640
which brings us to rare sugars
like allulose, which are

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.720
natural sweeteners with low or
zero calories and a sugar-like taste.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.560
For those of you who
haven't heard of allulose,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.440
it's been through
a bit of rebranding.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.360
Just like rapeseed oil morphed
into canola, forcing municipalities

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.320
to update their town slogans,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.800
allulose used to be called psicose,
a sugar you may not want to eat

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:51.440
in the shower.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.360
The name "psicose" is derived
from the antibiotic from which

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.520
it was isolated --- a strange
origin story for a sweetener.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.840
In the Frontiers of Bioengineering
and Biotechnology, emphasis

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.120
is placed on the fact that
allulose is a real and natural sugar

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.760
rather than artificial, but ironically
if you look at a reference they cite,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.680
it talks about how allulose
is made, via functionalized

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.400
polyhydroxyalkanoate nano-beads

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.680
decorated with D-tagatose
3-epimerase (DTE)

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.600
produced in recombinant
endotoxin-free branded bacteria.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.440
And now technically allulose is a
natural sugar, a so-called rare sugar,

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.120
which have been defined by the
International Society of Rare Sugars

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.040
as sugars that are present
in limited quantities in nature.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.000
But recent technological advances,
such as enzymatic engineering

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.120
using genetically modified
microorganisms, now allow

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.000
manufacturers to produce
otherwise rare sugars

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.160
like allulose in
substantial quantities.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.160
But small amounts have
pre-existed in the food supply,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.240
so the FDA granted it generally
recognized as safe status,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.960
but it's been like really
small amounts, like a few

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.920
dozen milligrams, or a quarter of
a quarter of a quarter of a teaspoon.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.720
See, allulose is generated
when fructose is heated.

00:03:17.101 --> 00:03:19.360
And allulose is
created incidentally

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.240
in the process by which high
fructose corn syrup is made.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.320
So basing the safety of allulose
on the fact that it's already been

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.560
present in the food supply
isn't very convincing.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.360
You don't know if it's safe
until you put it to the test.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.640
Unlike table sugar, allulose
is safe for the teeth;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.600
isn't apparently metabolized
by cavity-causing bacteria

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.160
to make acid and
build up plaque.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.120
It doesn't raise blood sugars,
even in diabetics.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.320
It can be labeled as having
zero calories, though technically

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.640
it may have like one and a half
calories per teaspoon,

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.480
similar to what you
see with erythritol.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.680
Also like erythritol, it's only about
70% as sweet as table sugar

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.680
but has almost the same
taste, performance,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.760
and texture as regular sugar.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.000
Are there any toxicities?

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.480
Allulose is considered
a relatively nontoxic sugar.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:14.720
What does that mean?

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.880
Well, in pets, we know a
sweetener called xylitol can

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.440
be extremely dangerous to dogs.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.080
As little as a half teaspoon in a
30-pound dog can be life-threatening,

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.440
whereas a similar dose of allulose
apparently wouldn't be a problem,

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.120
though like over half cup at
a time could make them sick.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.120
This was a single dose
study though.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.920
The long-term safety of allulose
was tested over a period

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.240
of a few months and didn't seem to
cause any harmful effects at a dose

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.240
of about one and a half teaspoons
a day for healthy 30-pound dogs.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.640
In fact, their cholesterol
actually went down,

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.440
leading the investigators
to suggest clinical studies to see

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.320
if it's something vets should start
prescribing for overweight pooches.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.520
What about in people?

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.320
When the dose of allulose
was gradually increased

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.320
to identify the maximum single
dose for occasional ingestion,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.000
no cases of severe gastrointestinal
symptoms were noted

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.720
until a dose of 0.4 g/kg of
bodyweight was reached,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.000
which could be about 7 teaspoons,
with severe symptoms

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.760
of diarrhea noted once you
get up around 9 teaspoons.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.240
And you say, "who could eat that
much sugar at a time anyway?"

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.600
A single can of Coke
has 10 teaspoons,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.240
and a bottle of Mountain Dew
has nearly twice that.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.680
So allulose is clearly not suitable
as a standalone sweetener

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.880
for sugary beverages.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.320
In terms of the daily upper limit
given in smaller doses

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.480
throughout the day, once you
hit around 17 teaspoons a day,

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.480
depending on your weight,
people start getting

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.200
severe nausea, abdominal
pain, headache, diarrhea.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.840
So people should probably
stay under single doses

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.520
of about 7 teaspoons,

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.320
and not exceed around
15 for the whole day.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.600
The average American may
be getting 17 teaspoons

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.600
of added sugar a day,
so it's certainly not something

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.400
the food industry can
completely switch over to.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.880
In this way, allulose is close
to xylitol in terms of

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.240
maximal single dose,

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.960
whereas the average-weight man
in the US could get away with

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.760
14 teaspoons of
erythritol at a time,

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.760
and the average woman,
15 teaspoons.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.000
Still not enough to sweeten
a bottle of Coke, but offers

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.080
a little more leeway
for sweetening tea

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.920
or sprinkling on a
grapefruit or something.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.240
Allulose is claimed to
provide health benefits

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.240
though, compared to erythritol.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.560
Forth generation sweeteners
like allulose are said to have

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.720
as an advantage:
additional functions.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.691
We'll find out if that's true, next.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.800
Allulose is a kind of low-calorie
sugar naturally existing

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.160
in very small quantities,

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.520
but now industrially produced
as a commercial sweetener,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.200
said to have advantages

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.480
that make it comparable to
erythritol as a sugar substitute.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.680
It's said to have
anti-diabetic effects,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.560
but this was in obese mice.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:07.680
Allulose decreases LDL cholesterol
levels in high fat-fed hamsters and

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.320
is said to have a substantial impact
on obesity in lard-munching mice,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.120
but what about men and women?

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.040
In a petri dish, allulose
inhibits fat cell precursors

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.120
from maturing into fat cells

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.826
and reduced the amount of
fat accumulation within fat cells.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.680
Therefore, the researchers
conclude, allulose may be

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.520
a promising sugar substitute
for an anti-obesity diet,

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.000
but you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.560
They gave people about a
teaspoon of allulose a half hour

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.960
before eating a meal, and compared
to the no-sugar control group,

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.360
the allulose group started
burning more fat.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.400
The researchers concluded
that allulose enhances

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.120
after-a-meal fat burning,
indicating that it could be

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.280
a novel sweetener
to control and maintain

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.840
healthy body weight through
enhanced energy metabolism.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.920
Okay, but first of all,
it was only 15 calories of fat

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.320
burned over that 4-hour period.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.480
And they didn't burn
more calories overall.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.440
They just switched from burning
carb calories to fat calories

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.800
and so may have just switched
back later on and made up

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.320
for it later in the day. You
can't just look at one meal.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.520
You need to track people's actual
weight over time, and here we go.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.320
A weight reducing effect of a syrup
that included about 5% allulose,

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.000
compared to high fructose corn syrup.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.480
The results show significant
decreases in body weight, body fat,

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.160
and waist circumference, but it was
some proprietary syrup mixture,

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:39.840
and look, anything would look good
against high fructose corn syrup.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:40.640
This is the study
I've been looking for.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.800
Evaluating the effect of allulose
for fat mass reduction in humans.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.160
Over a hundred individuals

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.800
randomized to a placebo
control -- sucralose --

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.200
or a teaspoon of allulose
twice a day,

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.840
or one-and-three-quarter
teaspoons twice a day,

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.920
and despite no change
of physical activity

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.240
or calorie consumption
between the two groups,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.200
body fat was significantly decreased
following allulose supplementation.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.520
They even took CT scans,
so they could tell where

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.920
the fat was disappearing from.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.240
Now, the drop in body fat was only
about 2 pounds over 12 weeks,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.720
and the drop in abdominal fat
in the higher dose group

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.160
was almost totally a drop
in subcutaneous fat,

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.080
the flabby superficial fat,
not the dangerous visceral fat

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.400
deeper down that
really matters for health.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.360
But still, a body fat loss despite
no change in calorie intake,

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.200
similar to what was
seen in the mice,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.920
but the hamster effect
did not materialize:

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.880
no significant change in LDL
cholesterol in either

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.240
of the allulose groups.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.640
What about the purported
anti-diabetes effects?

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.480
If you have people chug a beverage
with up to about 2 teaspoons

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:50.160
of allulose alone -- previously
known as psicose -- or with

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.880
a big load of rapidly-digesting carbs,
the allulose alone has predictably

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.240
no influence on blood glucose
or insulin concentration.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.080
Yet when you consume allulose
along with the refined carbs,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.840
there is actually a suppression of the
elevation of blood sugar and insulin,

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:09.520
and by a significant amount: a 30%
drop in blood sugar and insulin levels

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.680
over a two-hour period. We think it's
because the presence of allulose

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.120
impairs the absorption of sugar
through the intestinal lining,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.640
because they all might be competing
for the same sugar transporter.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:26.000
Yeah, but who's chugging maltodextrin?
What about just like a regular meal?

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.560
And here we go -- and
in borderline diabetics

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.360
who could use some blood
sugar spike suppression.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.680
A randomized double-blind placebo-
controlled crossover experiment

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.400
to see what drinking one and
a quarter teaspoon of allulose

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.480
in a cup of tea
with a meal would do,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.680
and indeed a significant drop,
though only about 15%.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.840
You can see how they
tried to exaggerate the y-axis.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.600
They also did a second experiment:
randomized people to a little over

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.040
a teaspoon of allulose three times
a day with meals for 12 weeks.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.120
Nice to see there didn't appear
to be any adverse side effects,

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.600
but they also didn't find
any weight loss effects,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.000
in contrast with that other study.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.560
So the body fat data [are] mixed
and so too [are] the sugar data.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.240
This study found no effects
of allulose on blood sugars

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.840
in healthy participants, though
a similar study on diabetics did.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:20.960
A systematic review and meta-analysis
of all such controlled feeding trials

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.360
suggest that the acute
benefits on blood sugars

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.600
was of borderline significance,

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.200
and it's unclear whether or
not this alone could translate

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.720
into meaningful improvements
in blood sugar control over time,

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.160
so maybe it's not enough
to just add allulose.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.600
You might actually have
to also cut out the junk.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.520
Although ability to suppress
blood sugar responses

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:48.240
is the most well studied
aspect of allulose, I do just want

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.000
to cover one last angle:
the fact that allulose extends

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.240
the lifespan of a tiny roundworm
called "C. elegans" through

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.600
a dietary restriction mechanism,
even though they ate

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.040
the same amount of food.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.880
Two months in, just a few percent
of the control group were still alive,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.760
whereas twice as many
in the low allulose group

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:11.360
and about 25% still around
in the higher allulose group.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:14.480
We think it worked boosting
the expression and activities

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.560
of antioxidant enzymes
in the body,

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.480
extending lifespan by
mopping up free radicals.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:25.360
But who cares about the
survival curves of worms?

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:29.680
Well, C. elegans is a well-studied
model of aging and longevity,

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.080
but mostly just for convenience sake,

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.760
but does share most of the longevity
genes or pathways we're interested in.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:41.760
It would be nice to see experiments on
at least human cells, and here we go.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.440
The effect of allulose on free
radicals and oxidative stress

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.480
in human coronary artery
endothelial cells --

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:50.560
the cells that line the
inside of our arteries.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:55.200
Add a bunch of sugar, and
free radical levels shoot up,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.920
but add the same amount of sugar
along with some allulose,

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.400
and it's as if you never
even added the sugar,

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.120
and this then translates
to inhibiting the expression

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.320
of an inflammatory marker

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.520
that plays a role in the buildup
of atherosclerotic plaque.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.960
However, it's still too
early to draw conclusions

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:15.040
about the clinical relevance
of these data. To conclude,

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.920
are these rare sugars like
allulose healthy alternatives

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.680
for traditional sweeteners?
Well, considering the variety

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.720
of potentially beneficial
effects of allulose

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:29.360
without known disadvantages from
metabolic and toxicological studies,

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.840
allulose may currently be the
most promising rare sugar,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.920
but how much is that saying?

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.480
We just don't have
a lot of good human data.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:41.360
As a result of the absence
of these studies,

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:46.261
it may be too early to recommend
rare sugars for human consumption.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.600
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.880
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:55.520
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.720
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.960
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.240
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:08.840
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:12.960
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

